<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this in the
    same commit as the tag.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="AddressAutocompleteAzure"
           version="0.0.2"
           display-name-key="AddressAutocompleteAzure_Name"
           description-key="AddressAutocompleteAzure_Desc"
           control-type="virtual">

    <!--
      THIS CONTROL IS PREMIUM, AND THAT IS THE PRICE OF WHAT IT DOES.

      It calls Azure Maps from the browser — `geocode:autocomplete` on every
      settled keystroke and `geocode` once on a pick — so `enabled="true"` is
      not a switch that could be left off. The consequence is a licensing one:
      every end user of an app containing this component needs a Power Apps
      licence rather than an Office 365 one, whether or not they ever open the
      field. `docs/limitations.md` says so in the first paragraph; nothing at
      build or install time will.

      Every host the control can be pointed at is listed, because the
      `endpoint` property below is an *enum* over exactly these hosts rather
      than free text: a maker choosing the European geographic endpoint has to
      find it declared here, and a domain a maker could type in but this list
      omits would be a request the declaration does not cover.

        atlas.microsoft.com        public cloud (the default)
        us. / eu.atlas.microsoft.com   geographic scope — data residency
        kr. / br.atlas.microsoft.com   the same, for Korea and Brazil
        atlas.azure.us             Azure Government

      `npm run check` warns on `enabled="true"` at all, which is the reminder
      to re-read this paragraph rather than a fault to fix.
    -->
    <external-service-usage enabled="true">
      <domain>atlas.microsoft.com</domain>
      <domain>us.atlas.microsoft.com</domain>
      <domain>eu.atlas.microsoft.com</domain>
      <domain>kr.atlas.microsoft.com</domain>
      <domain>br.atlas.microsoft.com</domain>
      <domain>atlas.azure.us</domain>
    </external-service-usage>

    <!--
      SEVEN BOUND PROPERTIES, AND THAT IS THE FEATURE.

      A field control attaches to its *first* bound property — the column the
      maker places it on — and renders every additional one as its own column
      picker in the configuration pane. `required="false"` does not suppress
      the picker. The skill names that as a cost and it is one: seven pickers
      is more than any control in the catalogue asks for (geo-stamp asks for
      three, the date range picker two).

      It is taken because an address *is* several columns, and the alternative
      — one bound column plus six output properties — makes every install a
      JavaScript job: outputs are written to whatever the maker wires them to
      and read back from nothing, so a form script would have to copy each one
      into its column on change. Binding them means a pick lands in the
      record's own columns and survives a reload with no code on the form.

      Only `addressLine1` is required. A maker who binds nothing else gets a
      street-line type-ahead that writes one column, which is a working
      control rather than a broken one. Which of the six are *mapped* is not
      visible from inside the control — an unmapped optional bound property
      and a mapped-but-empty one read the same — so `getOutputs()` emits a key
      only for a column it has a value for or has cleared after the platform
      supplied one. See `index.ts`, and SPEC.md for what the form has and has
      not confirmed about that.

      `latitude` and `longitude` are on a type group because Dataverse's
      `address1_latitude` / `address1_longitude` are Double, which the manifest
      calls `FP`, and a `Decimal`-only declaration made `pcf-geo-stamp`
      unbindable to them. `Whole.None` is deliberately excluded: a coordinate
      truncated to an integer is a wrong place, not an approximate one.
    -->
    <property name="addressLine1"
              display-name-key="AddressLine1_Name"
              description-key="AddressLine1_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="true" />

    <property name="city"
              display-name-key="City_Name"
              description-key="City_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <property name="stateOrProvince"
              display-name-key="StateOrProvince_Name"
              description-key="StateOrProvince_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <property name="postalCode"
              display-name-key="PostalCode_Name"
              description-key="PostalCode_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <property name="country"
              display-name-key="Country_Name"
              description-key="Country_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      The coordinates cost a second transaction per pick — `geocode:autocomplete`
      promises no position, so `geocode` is called with the picked address —
      and whether the maker bound the two columns below is not something the
      control can see. So the call is a switch, off by default: a TwoOptions
      input cannot be defaulted to on, which here is the right way round, and
      a maker who binds latitude and longitude turns it on having read what it
      buys. Bound but switched off, the two columns are left as they are.
    -->
    <property name="resolveCoordinates"
              display-name-key="ResolveCoordinates_Name"
              description-key="ResolveCoordinates_Desc"
              of-type="TwoOptions"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <type-group name="coordinate">
      <type>FP</type>
      <type>Decimal</type>
    </type-group>

    <property name="latitude"
              display-name-key="Latitude_Name"
              description-key="Latitude_Desc"
              of-type-group="coordinate"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <property name="longitude"
              display-name-key="Longitude_Name"
              description-key="Longitude_Desc"
              of-type-group="coordinate"
              usage="bound"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      The Azure Maps subscription key. Required, and no default: a control
      with no key makes no request and says so under the field.

      **Anyone who can open the form designer can read this.** It is the
      account's shared key, which Azure calls a master key, and it is sent
      from the browser with every request — in the `subscription-key` HEADER,
      never in the URL, so it does not land in a browser history or a proxy
      log. `docs/installation.md` tells makers to give the control its own
      Azure Maps account and to restrict that account's CORS allowed-origins
      to the organisation URL, which is Azure's own guidance for a key held by
      a browser. A SAS token would be the tighter answer and cannot be a form
      property: it expires within 24 hours.
    -->
    <property name="subscriptionKey"
              display-name-key="SubscriptionKey_Name"
              description-key="SubscriptionKey_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="true" />

    <!--
      Which Azure Maps host to call — an enum over the domains declared at the
      top of this file, so the two cannot disagree. `default-value` is right
      here: the platform has no answer of its own to override, and the public
      cloud is what an account is in unless somebody chose a geography.
    -->
    <property name="endpoint"
              display-name-key="Endpoint_Name"
              description-key="Endpoint_Desc"
              of-type="Enum"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="public">
      <value name="public" display-name-key="Endpoint_Public_Name">public</value>
      <value name="us" display-name-key="Endpoint_US_Name">us</value>
      <value name="eu" display-name-key="Endpoint_EU_Name">eu</value>
      <value name="kr" display-name-key="Endpoint_KR_Name">kr</value>
      <value name="br" display-name-key="Endpoint_BR_Name">br</value>
      <value name="gov" display-name-key="Endpoint_Gov_Name">gov</value>
    </property>

    <!--
      Where to look. Azure Maps requires a location signal on every
      autocomplete call — `coordinates` or a `bbox` — and ranks by it.

      `countryRegion` is an ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 code and *restricts* results to
      that country; the bias point *ranks* results near it. Both optional, and
      NO `default-value` on either: unset means the control sends a
      world-sized bounding box and every country, which always works and
      which a maker narrows once they know their users. A default of any
      particular country would be a wrong answer somewhere.
    -->
    <property name="countryRegion"
              display-name-key="CountryRegion_Name"
              description-key="CountryRegion_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <property name="biasLatitude"
              display-name-key="BiasLatitude_Name"
              description-key="BiasLatitude_Desc"
              of-type="FP"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <property name="biasLongitude"
              display-name-key="BiasLongitude_Name"
              description-key="BiasLongitude_Desc"
              of-type="FP"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      How a suggestion is written into the region and country columns.
      `adminDistricts[0]` arrives with both `name` ("Washington") and
      `shortName` ("WA"); `countryRegion` with `name` and `ISO`. Which spelling
      a table wants is the maker's convention, not the control's, so each is a
      switch with the commoner spelling as the declared default.
    -->
    <property name="regionFormat"
              display-name-key="RegionFormat_Name"
              description-key="RegionFormat_Desc"
              of-type="Enum"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="short">
      <value name="short" display-name-key="RegionFormat_Short_Name">short</value>
      <value name="long" display-name-key="RegionFormat_Long_Name">long</value>
    </property>

    <property name="countryFormat"
              display-name-key="CountryFormat_Name"
              description-key="CountryFormat_Desc"
              of-type="Enum"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="name">
      <value name="name" display-name-key="CountryFormat_Name_Name">name</value>
      <value name="iso" display-name-key="CountryFormat_ISO_Name">iso</value>
    </property>

    <!--
      Two numbers with no `default-value`, on the rule every control here
      follows: unset is a state the code can read, and the defaults (three
      characters before the first request, five suggestions) live beside the
      code that applies them rather than in a manifest attribute that arrives
      as a real value from a maker who never touched it.
    -->
    <property name="minCharacters"
              display-name-key="MinCharacters_Name"
              description-key="MinCharacters_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <property name="maxSuggestions"
              display-name-key="MaxSuggestions_Name"
              description-key="MaxSuggestions_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <property name="placeholder"
              display-name-key="Placeholder_Name"
              description-key="Placeholder_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="input"
              required="false" />

    <!--
      The one thing a pick produces that no column holds: the address as
      Azure Maps formats it, in one line. A canvas app reads it directly; a
      form script gets it from the control's output on change. An output
      rather than an eighth bound column, because it is derived from the
      seven and a column holding it would drift the moment one of them is
      edited by hand.
    -->
    <property name="formattedAddress"
              display-name-key="FormattedAddress_Name"
              description-key="FormattedAddress_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/AddressAutocompleteAzure.css" order="1" />
      <platform-library name="React" version="16.14.0" />
      <platform-library name="Fluent" version="9.46.2" />
      <!--
        English only, which is a choice rather than an omission: every key
        added here is a key owed to each language listed, and a stale
        translation is worse than an honest fallback. Add a language ABOVE
        this line — `pcf-start` reads whichever file is listed last.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/AddressAutocompleteAzure.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      No feature-usage. The only platform surfaces this control touches are
      the bound properties and `context.mode` / `userSettings` / `resources`,
      none of which is gated; the Azure Maps calls are a browser `fetch`,
      which the external-service declaration above covers.
    -->
  </control>
</manifest>
